{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 proposes 'targeted checklist-equivalent perturbations' as the diagnostic, but explicitly concedes in its own overturn conditions that 'No checklist-equivalent perturbation can be constructed that is both valid and discriminating within a single evaluation.' This is not a merely hypothetical defeater: the problem demands evidence available 'within a single evaluation' of a task with objectively verifiable outcomes. For such tasks, the correct answer is fixed by external fact; altering the item to create a perturbation either changes the verified outcome (making it a different task, not a perturbation of the same evaluation) or preserves the outcome while only superficially changing wording, in which case the 'checklist-equivalent' substitution is not decision-relevant and cannot distinguish compliance from reliability. SRC_001's own analogy disanalogy reinforces this: human-judgment perturbations are subjective and confounded. Thus the proposed discriminating evidence is self-undermining for the class of tasks the problem specifies.","target":"1404d6b292e7c13d330aaa5a6dedc4c2938fd0cdeba6ea3df6506a4fd69ab433"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 requires that panel disagreement predict incorrectness, but for checklist-driven panels this signal is only informative conditional on the checklist mapping being imperfect. Under the exact problem setting (explicit checklist, verifiable outcomes), a well-designed checklist induces a deterministic or near-deterministic item→answer mapping; panel disagreement then reflects rater noise (attention, training variance), not task-relevant uncertainty. SRC_002's own countercondition concedes this: 'The checklist forces a binary choice on each item, eliminating the possibility of calibrated redundancy.' Since explicit checklists for verifiable-outcome tasks typically do force discrete feature→decision steps, the central diagnostic is undefined for the paradigmatic case. Worse, SRC_002 offers no within-evaluation way to separate disagreement caused by genuine item difficulty from disagreement caused by checklist ambiguity or shared confusion; both produce the same observed signal, but only the former distinguishes reliability, so the proposed evidence does not validly distinguish genuine gain from compliance under the problem's constraints.","target":"4be7d4709a81036e14bd1a228db6179eeb6636b8fd3153bcbf5af71b8415643b"}]}